    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d184">

                <pb facs="0440" n="316" sameAs="frus:frus1881/pg316"/>

                <head>No. 183.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Blaine to Mr. Angell</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1881-10-28"><hi rendition="#italic">October</hi> 28,
                        1881</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 128.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have to acknowledge the receipt of your
                    dispatch No. 204, of August 28 last, reporting the decision of the Chinese
                    Government in the matter of the Yang-tse junk regulations, and to say that, in
                    view of all the facts now before it, the Department is not supposed to question
                    the justice of the conclusion reached by that government.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>JAMES G. BLAINE.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
